---
source: tests/cli_config.rs
expression: text(&out.stdout)
---
Render a Markdown document, or show an image, in the terminal

Usage: kiri [OPTIONS] [FILE]...

Arguments:
  [FILE]...
          Markdown file to render, a CSV or TSV file to show as a table, an image or a Mermaid
          diagram (.mmd) to show on its own, or a directory to show its README. Several files are
          shown one after another, each under a header that names it; one that cannot be shown is
          reported, and the others are still shown. Without one, or with -, kiri reads standard
          input unless it is a terminal; - can be given only once

Options:
      --doctor
          Show what kiri finds out about the terminal and which settings it uses, as Markdown when
          written to a file, for a bug report

      --list-emojis
          List the emoji shortcodes, such as :smile:. A terminal shows them as a table; a pipe or a
          file gets one line per emoji

      --raw
          With --doctor: the report as Markdown also in a terminal, without its sample, to copy into
          a bug report

      --format <FORMAT>
          What the file is: markdown, csv, tsv, mermaid (a diagram shown on its own), text (the file
          as it is, without rendering it), image, or an image format such as svg, png or jpg. By
          default its extension and content tell

      --width <WIDTH>
          Width in cells; defaults to the terminal width

      --mode <MODE>
          Which terminal features to use. Colours stay off when NO_COLOR is set

          Possible values:
          - auto:  Ask the terminal; plain text in a pipe
          - plain: Text only, no escape sequences
          - ansi:  Colours and attributes, no kitty extensions
          - kitty: All kitty features, without asking the terminal
          
          [default: auto]

      --theme <THEME>
          Theme file; overrides --light, --dark, KIRI_THEME and the configuration file

      --light
          A light page in greys, whatever the terminal's colours; code colours for a light
          background

      --dark
          A dark page in greys, whatever the terminal's colours; code colours for a dark background

  -M, --no-margins
          No margins, heading indents or code block padding, for copying text

  -I, --no-images
          Show images as their alt text, without reading them, and diagrams as code. An image or
          diagram file given on its own is still shown

      --images
          Show images, and diagrams unless they are off, whatever KIRI_NO_IMAGES and the
          configuration file say

      --paging <WHEN>
          When to show the document in the pager: always, auto (when it is longer than the window
          and the output is a terminal) or never
          
          [possible values: always, auto, never]

  -p
          Show the document in the pager: --paging=always

  -P
          Print the document without the pager: --paging=never

      --follow <SWITCH>
          Follow a lone file in the pager: on (read it again whenever it changes, with the view kept
          where it is) or off; off unless set. F turns it on and off in the pager
          
          [possible values: on, off]

  -f
          Follow the file: --follow=on

  -F
          Do not follow the file: --follow=off

      --front-matter <STYLE>
          Front matter render style: table, code (a code block), hide, or off (read as Markdown);
          table unless set. Front matter is the metadata block at the start of a Markdown file
          
          [possible values: table, code, hide, off]

      --table-rows <STYLE>
          Table row style: zebra (every second row shaded where the terminal's colours are known),
          lines, indent (the lines after the first of a wrapped value start two cells in), underline
          (each row's last line underlined), or off; zebra unless set
          
          [possible values: off, zebra, lines, indent, underline]

      --diagrams <SWITCH>
          Diagrams in code blocks, such as Mermaid: on (drawn as images) or off (shown as code); on
          unless set
          
          [possible values: on, off]

      --render-errors <WHERE>
          Where to tell why a diagram could not be drawn: inline (in the document, below its code),
          stderr (a warning) or both; both unless set
          
          [possible values: inline, stderr, both]

      --max-image-size <MB>
          Largest image file to read, in megabytes (at most 100); larger images show their alt text
          
          [default: 10]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

Images and diagrams are drawn only in terminals that support kitty's graphics protocol with Unicode
placeholders, such as kitty 0.28 or later; elsewhere images show their alt text and diagrams their
code.
